WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.500
The ICU is really where you
go when you're very sick.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.350
It's a really intense
interventionist environment.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.180
And so, when you're
in the ICU, you really

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.130
need to know how acutely
ill an individual is

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:18.660
so you can decide whether you
need to specify treatments

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.220
very quickly, or
whether you need

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.170
to focus more care on them.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.210
There is a tremendous
amount of information

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.910
that the doctors and nurses
and all of the care staff

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.690
are actually encoding in some
of these notes that they take.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.420
And a lot of that is
just harder to access.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.160
It's not a nice set of codes
that are already presorted

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.350
in some very easy-to-use way.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.110
It's just sort of this free
text that's a sprawl and very

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.340
difficult to use.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.880
But probably contains
more informative things

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.070
about the underlying
patient state.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.860
So there's patient and
he's really, really sick.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.500
And then there's a doctor,
and she's totally overwhelmed.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.690
Because patients
generates so much data,

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.870
there's basically a zillion
notes and constant signals who

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.050
can keep up with 1,000 hertz.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.850
Oh, yeah, I guess there's also
that other stuff, like age

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.890
and gender and acuity scores.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.110
Seems pretty important
to think about.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.520
And maybe that would all be OK
if there was just one patient,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.590
but there are so, so many
patients in a modern ICU.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.500
So here's what this
doctor really wants.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.090
She'd like a real-time indicator
of how sick each patient is.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:29.100
And let's see on a scale of
faking it to dead, and maybe

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.090
beyond?

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.920
But how can she get that?

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.220
That's where we come in.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.795
We can predict how
sick patients are

00:01:34.795 --> 00:01:37.170
by looking at their notes,
which is great because there's

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:42.780
a lot of notes, nearly half a
million, from 19,308 patients.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.920
Using a couple of magical,
fancy-looking plates

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.530
we can model common topics
that we find in the notes.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:52.260
And then, for each
patient, we can figure out

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.810
which topics are the
most relevant to them.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.367
Then you say, hey,
I feel like you're

00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:58.950
saying a lot of words
that are related

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.770
to the topic of septic shock.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:05.640
You may not think your patient
is going through septic shock,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.710
but maybe they're going
to at some point soon.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.620
So maybe you should take
a look at them right now,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:14.460
or try some more interventionist
techniques at this point.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.730
If you look at our population,
the baseline mortality

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.560
is about 11%.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.570
But what we can see is
the relative mortality

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.820
of the topics is pretty
different from that.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.515
Some are really
high, like this one

00:02:26.515 --> 00:02:28.890
that looks like it's about
respiratory failure with words

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:31.594
like intubated and
vents, secretions.

00:02:31.594 --> 00:02:33.510
Definitely not a topic
you want in your notes.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.610
But some are really
low, like this one

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.680
that looks like it's
about broken bones.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.035
Broken bones hurt, but they
don't usually kill you.

00:02:40.035 --> 00:02:42.600
And even better,
we can look at how

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.920
the topics change in time for
each new note that comes in,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.360
which is great because
usually the information

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.240
that a doctor gets at
admission, that baseline data,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.290
is less and less
predictive over time.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.290
Since the notes accumulate,
they don't start off

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.050
doing that well, but they
get more and more predictive.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.430
And when we use both of
them together and combine

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:07.170
the models, it helps us
get a prediction that's

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.260
great from the time the
patient shows up to the time

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.040
that they leave the hospital.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.750
Everything I hear about now
related to clinical care

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:20.040
is all about, can we make
more space for doctors

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.460
to be doctors and
less room for them

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.240
to be accountants, and
note takers, and, you know,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.230
transcribists?

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.610
Or, whatever it is, right?

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.280
We want to allow
doctors to actually feel

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.990
like they're making decisions
about a patient's care.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:35.850
Right?

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.070
And what I would hope
is, if a system like this

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:41.730
really was implemented, it
would allow doctors to spend

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.270
more time caring for patients.

